616588991e-07,-9.536738616588991e-07],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,4340,1657,4727,31340,525,9363,315,400,17,1124,15136,220,19,1124,15136,220,21,1124,15136,220,23,1124,15136,220,16,15,1124,15136,220,16,17,46311,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nHow many perfect squares are factors of $2 \\times 4 \\times 6 \\times 8 \\times 10 \\times 12?$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"b34030bdca4f9c8c74ba519a8824f8822d480d5c317d871a0f9b43b2a1a170e5"},"seconds":13.796724319458008,"seed":919338609433988744,"stop":"eos","text":"To determine how many perfect squares are factors of \\(2 \\times 4 \\times 6 \\times 8 \\times 10 \\times 12\\), we start by finding the prime factorization of the product.\n\nFirst, we factorize each of the numbers:\n\\[2 = 2\\]\n\\[4 = 2^2\\]\n\\[6 = 2 \\times 3\\]\n\\[8 = 2^3\\]\n\\[10 = 2 \\times 5\\]\n\\[12 = 2^2 \\times 3\\]\n\nNow, we multiply these factorizations together:\n\\[2 \\times 4 \\times 6 \\times 8 \\times 10 \\times 12 = 2 \\times 2^2 \\times (2 \\times 3) \\times 2^3 \\times (2 \\times 5) \\times (2^2 \\times 3)\\]\nCombining the powers of 2, we get:\n\\[2^1 \\times 2^2 \\times 2^1 \\times 2^3 \\times 2^1 \\times 2^2 = 2^{1+2+1+3+1+2} = 2^{10}\\]\nAnd combining the powers of 3 and 5, we get:\n\\[3^1 \\times 3^1 = 3^2\\]\n\\[5^1 = 5^1\\]\nSo the prime factorization of the product is:\n\\[2^{10} \\times 3^2 \\times 5^1\\]\n\nA perfect square must have even exponents in its prime factorization. Therefore, we need to count the number of ways to choose even exponents for the primes 2, 3, and 5 in the factorization \\(2^{10} \\times 3^2 \\times 5^1\\).\n\nFor the prime 2, the possible even exponents are 0, 2, 4, 6, 8, and 10 (6 choices).\nFor the prime 3, the possible even exponents are 0 and 2 (2 choices).\nFor the prime 5, the possible even exponents are 0 (1 choice).\n\nThe total number of perfect square factors is the product of the number of choices for each prime:\n\\[6 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 12\\]\n\nThus, the number of perfect square factors of \\(2 \\times 4 \\times 6 \\times 8 \\times 10 \\times 12\\) is \\(\\boxed{12}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{12}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"number_theory:481"}
